      "problem": "Evaluate $\\dfrac {5!} {2!}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $5! = 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2! \\, .$ Then, we can cancel the $2!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {5!} {2!} \n&= \\dfrac {5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2!} {2!} \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac {5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot  \\cancel{2!}} {\\cancel{2!}} \\[5pt]\n&= \t\t\\dfrac {5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3} {1} \\[5pt]\n&= \n\t\t60\n\t}\n$$",
